These questions will not be visible to all attendees once submitted, but we will try to get to as many as possible during the Q&A portion of the meeting. During the small group discussions, the chat will be visible to everyone, and we encourage you to keep any comments in the chat respectful to other attendees. If you use inappropriate language, you will be removed from the meeting. I'd now like to introduce Andrew McFarlane from the MBTA, who will first introduce the MBTA general manager and then begin the main presentation.

11
00:04:42,704 --> 00:05:03,089
Andrew. Thank you, Jason. Good evening, everyone. Thank you for joining us tonight for our Bus Network Redesign meeting. Tonight we'll be diving into the communities of Brookline, Cambridge, Medford, Somerville, and Watertown, and what the MBTA's Bus Network Redesign proposal can mean for these municipalities.

12
00:05:04,169 --> 00:05:29,583
For tonight's meeting, we will have a presentation that I will co-lead with my colleague, Melissa Dulay, who's a senior director of service planning for the MBTA. After we review the proposal and the presentation, we'll break into small group discussions where you'll have an opportunity to talk with MBTA project team staff, review the proposals closely and provide more feedback.

13
00:05:30,663 --> 00:05:45,766
We'll then reconvene as a larger group, where you'll have an opportunity for questions and answers if you didn't get any of your questions answered in the smaller groups. Before we get started, I want to introduce the MBTA General Manager Steve Poptak to give some opening remarks. General Manager.

14
00:05:46,778 --> 00:06:08,682
Thanks so much, Andrew. I'm here live from Mattapan station, one of our really important nodes with both BUS and obviously the Mattapan trolley. And I want to thank everyone who joined us today. I want to welcome everyone who's joining us for this important conversation about the future of our BUS network.

15
00:06:09,222 --> 00:06:38,787
As you know, as everyone on this call knows, transit is really essential to this region's economy and our bus system serves our most transit dependent populations. And while this bus system has grown over the years, this area has experienced such dynamic change. We need a bus service that changes with it. And we're really excited to share this draft network map with you. We really view this process as a once in a generation opportunity to make

16
00:06:39,243 --> 00:07:05,838
bold improvements to the MBTA's bus network and to do it for the people who depend on us most. So this is not only a redesign of the bus system, but to be clear, it's also increasing the amount of bus service that we are delivering. The plan over the next five years is to increase bus service by 25% and that includes a 70% increase on weekends. It also means

17
00:07:06,479 --> 00:07:26,324
doubling the amount of routes that have high frequency service. And that's that's what we define as a bus stopping every 15 minutes or less throughout the service day. Now these changes can sometimes be difficult. There can sometimes be tradeoffs. But I believe the benefits to improving our bus system are really clear.

18
00:07:26,645 --> 00:07:46,625
So I thank you for your participation tonight. We're really excited to hear from all of you, particularly our bus riders. And I just want to reemphasize this draft network map is just that. It's a draft. We're really looking to make changes and hear your feedback and make this system as good as it can possibly be for our riders.

19
00:07:46,625 --> 00:08:12,747
So again, thank you for joining. Thank you for engaging in this process. And thank you for caring about the bus system here and our bus riders. So with that, I will hand it back to Andrew and turn off my mic here. I know it's a little noisy behind me, but I appreciate the opportunity to say hello to you all. Thank you, General Manager, for those exciting live remarks.

20
00:08:13,237 --> 00:08:42,835
I can't think of a better place to do that than live at our bus stop. Before we get started, we want to do a quick poll just to get a sense of who's in the room. Jason, could you activate the poll, please? So a poll should be popping up on your screen. We're asking folks, how regularly did you ride the bus and whether you've ridden the bus in the last month? So we'll give folks a few seconds to fill that out.

21
00:09:06,443 --> 00:09:31,908
Awesome, it looks like most folks have filled it out. So let's wrap up the poll. And it looks like, according to this, about 84% of you are regular bus riders, which is great. Really glad to see there's so many bus riders in the room tonight. And for the 16% of you who are maybe not regular bus riders, we hope that you are future riders under this new system. So with that, let's get started with the presentation.

22
00:09:33,612 --> 00:10:03,228
So what is bus network redesign? We know that the MBTA service area has changed a lot in the last few decades. New destinations and districts have emerged, areas like the Kendall Square area, Longwood Medical Area, Seaport District, Assembly Square. And we know that our bus system hasn't always met the needs of these growing districts. We also know that travel patterns have changed a lot in the last few years, especially in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic.

23
00:10:03,464 --> 00:10:21,503
And we want to make sure that our bus system really meets the needs of folks across the region, but especially those who need the service the most. So bus network redesign is just that, our effort to holistically reimagine the MBTA bus network and making sure that we're meeting the needs of current and future riders over the next few years.

24
00:10:25,334 --> 00:10:54,359
The bus network redesign is just one in a suite of initiatives that the MBTA is undertaking to really improve bus service and transform it for bus riders over the next few years. In addition to rethinking our bus routes, we are undertaking a major fleet and facilities program where we're looking to renovate and expand our bus garages and maintenance facilities. As part of that, we're also looking to transition to an electric fleet, making sure that we have the buses that we need to run more service.

25
00:10:55,726 --> 00:11:17,258
Additionally, we're working closely with cities and towns and other roadway owners to implement more bus priority on congested roadways. We know that a lot of our buses get stuck in traffic, and so we want to make sure that we're working with municipalities to put in bus lanes, traffic signal upgrades called transit signal priority, and other design upgrades.

26
00:11:17,258 --> 00:11:46,030
We've worked closely with many of the municipalities, especially the ones that are included in this meeting tonight, to do just that, to add more bus lanes, to make sure that we're getting the reliable service that we're looking for. Additionally, we're looking to improve the accessibility of bus stops, add more bus shelters, and there's another initiative underway to improve our fare collection system through fare transformation, where we're looking to move towards a system that would allow for all-door boarding.

27
00:11:50,553 --> 00:12:19,612
So with this proposed network, we're really trying to achieve five goals. First and foremost, we're trying to improve, make sure that our bus system is meeting the needs of those who need it the most, and making sure that we're concentrating those needs in areas where there are high rates of low income populations, people of color, seniors, people with disabilities, and people who live in households without access to a car. So I'll make sure that we're delivering the biggest benefits for these populations.

28
00:12:20,590 --> 00:12:32,757
Secondly, we're trying to provide a lot more frequent service. Time and time again, we hear from our riders, they want to see more frequent services process system. So like the general manager said, we're looking to double those in the coming years.

29
00:12:34,293 --> 00:12:56,838
We're also trying to provide a lot more all-day service. Traditionally, we've been very focused on trying to get folks to work at 9 a.m. and getting them home from work at 5 p.m. But in reality, we know that folks have a lot of different trip needs. Folks want to see more midday service, so they get to appointments. They want to have service later in the evening, so they can go see friends or get home from work later.

30
00:12:56,838 --> 00:13:06,338
We're also trying to provide a lot more service on the weekends. Many parts of our system don't have Sunday service, so we're trying to provide more seven-day service system-wide.

31
00:13:07,655 --> 00:13:31,229
Fourth, we're trying to provide new connections to more places. So I mentioned some of those growing areas earlier, the Seaport District, Assembly Square, Longwood Medical Area. We know that folks want to get to these areas, but there are also a lot of other areas, shopping plazas, community centers, other housing complexes that folks need to get to. So we're trying to provide more connections to places like that.

32
00:13:32,630 --> 00:13:49,808
Fifth, and finally, we're trying to provide a system that's simpler and easier to use. A lot of our routes have variations where they might have one type of trip at different times of the day, or they might not have service on the weekends. So we're trying to provide more consistent service seven days a week, 20 hours a day.

33
00:13:54,314 --> 00:14:18,310
So with this proposal, we are committed to a 25% increase in bus service hours over the next five years. This is really an unprecedented commitment to expand bus service, and we're really excited about this. We think this will have a major impact for how people travel in and around the Boston area in the years to come. Included in this 25% increase is a 70% increase in weekend service alone.

34
00:14:18,479 --> 00:14:37,649
And all this increase in service really allows us to serve many, many more riders. We estimated about 300,000 new residents would be within walking distance. That's within a quarter mile or so of a new high frequency service. That's a bus that comes every 15 minutes or better, seven days a week, 20 hours a day.

35
00:14:39,168 --> 00:14:58,017
In the Longwood Medical Area alone, we estimate that about 200,000 more residents will be within walking distance of a new high-frequency service that connects directly to the Longwood Medical Area. It also allows us to provide a lot more service to our equity populations, making sure that our bus system is providing the public good that it's intended to.

36
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:28,747
As you mentioned earlier, we are proposing a doubling of high-frequency service system-wide. Today, we have about 15 routes that would be considered high-frequency service. These are often called our key bus routes. They include buses like the number 1, the 77, 71, the 66. These are some of our most frequent and most popular routes system-wide. And we know that folks want to see a lot more services like those.

37
00:15:28,747 --> 00:15:44,727
So in our new system, we are proposing going from 15 corridors today to 30 corridors that would be considered high frequency in the future. This means that half of the service that we would be providing would be high frequency service, which we're very excited about.

38
00:15:45,233 --> 00:16:07,778
This also allows us to add some municipalities to the high-frequency map that weren't historically included in that map. Those include places like Everett, Lynn, Medford, Somerville, and many parts of Austin. We're also looking to provide more crosstown connections to connect folks to and from these high-frequency services, and also make sure they can get to and from rapid transit more easily.

39
00:16:11,947 --> 00:16:22,105
So with that, I will pass it off to my colleague, Melissa Joulet, who's the Senior Director of Service Planning for the MBTA. Melissa. Thanks for that introduction, Andrew.

40
00:16:23,607 --> 00:16:44,583
I'll start here with the map and describe the changes that we have coming for the north half of Brookline. I apologize, these are excerpted from some other materials that we've prepared. So this does include Austin and Brighton, but those were covered in our Boston area meeting, so I'll only be addressing the Brookline North changes.

41
00:16:44,583 --> 00:17:08,731
Another bookkeeping thing here, you'll see that we have identified some bus routes here with the nomenclature. The working name, we have the letter T and a number. That's what we're using to indicate any of the high frequency bus services. Those are the routes that we were talking about that are proposed to arrive every 15 minutes or better, seven days a week.

42
00:17:08,731 --> 00:17:35,562
for 20 hours a day. We were trying to come up with a way to say these are different from other routes in that you many times wouldn't even need a schedule to be able to use them. You can sort of show up with the expectation that within a reasonable amount of time, a bus would be along. Though the nomenclature of the T66 or the T70, that might be updated. So that's kind of our working name.

43
00:17:35,866 --> 00:17:51,340
For now, for reference, we were riffing off of the, you know, taking the MBTA because we want to make sure that bus is considered part of the network. Technically, these are all the frequent routes, but F routes sounded so, had sort of a negative connotation.

44
00:17:51,340 --> 00:18:19,252
So we're very excited to show that Brookline North, specifically the area getting on the approach to Longwood Medical Area, has many new high-frequency connections that are added to the map. There are already some existing connections, so you can see a route like the T57, which is pretty much identical to today's 57 bus. So actually, even for existing key bus routes like the 57,

45
00:18:19,252 --> 00:18:43,450
We are proposing to improve the off-peak frequency to be every 15 minutes or better, while today those routes are every 20 minutes or better. So even for routes where we don't have any route structure changes proposed, like the 57, we are suggesting that the late night service, for example, would improve from every 15 minutes to every, sorry, from every 20 minutes to every 15 minutes instead.

46
00:18:44,142 --> 00:18:59,718
Similarly, the T-66 is largely unchanged, with the exception of a very small reroute. Instead of from Brookline Village traveling directly to Brigham Circle, we're proposing that it would turn up Brookline Ave.

47
00:18:59,718 --> 00:19:15,192
and cut through the Longwood medical area to get a little bit better distribution into the Longwood medical area too. There's many places that folks are going for employment trips and also to opportunities for patient care.

48
00:19:15,192 --> 00:19:43,610
Though I'll note that any of the routings in the immediate vicinity of the Longwood medical area are subject to be updated. We have a deep dive study that we are keeping up with our MassDOT team to kind of look more closely at opportunities for bus priority, where we can put in bus layovers and other infrastructure improvements in a very busy and congested area.

49
00:19:45,061 --> 00:20:13,647
We also have some other new additions. For example, you can see there's a route called the T15 that starts at Oak Square, goes to Brighton Center, and continues down Washington Street straight through Brookline. That is promoting what's today's number 65 bus, and actually extending that a little bit farther, because today the 65 starts in Brighton Center. So this would become a seven-day, every 15 minute or better, service.

50
00:20:13,647 --> 00:20:32,176
It's very much used by folks in Brookline to connect to the Longwood medical area for commute trips. Today it doesn't have Sunday service so this is actually a big increase in seven day service with new Sunday service available as well. And the reason it's called the T15 is because it's

51
00:20:32,176 --> 00:20:53,422
mashed up with the number 15 bus that continues from Ruggles Station over to Nubian Square across Dudley Street and eventually would continue all the way to Fields Corner. So this becomes a sort of crosstown linking together Alston and Brighton, Brookline, Longwood Medical Area and parts of Roxbury as well.

52
00:20:56,982 --> 00:21:26,125
There's some other changes besides those high-frequency changes with the thick blue lines. You can see the darker brown lines or the lighter brown lines. Those are our medium and our low-frequency use. Some key things to note, we have the number 86 bus, which you can see starts at Reservoir, sort of provides a crosstown connection up through Austin and connects over to Harvard. It will actually terminate at Harvard. Today, it continues past Harvard.

53
00:21:26,125 --> 00:21:42,764
But the other half of the 86 is being merged with another route. So the 86, as we know, would be truncated to only operate between Reservoir and Harvard, with a transfer available for anyone continuing on to say Union Square in Somerville or Sullivan.

54
00:21:44,215 --> 00:22:03,031
There's also an extension proposed to the Route 60. Today it terminates at the mall at Chestnut Hill. We're proposing that that would be extended a little bit farther into Newton to Newton Center to get a little extra distribution to Langley Road and some other locations there.

55
00:22:04,516 --> 00:22:30,892
And lastly here, the Route 51 does not have any specific routing changes, but it would gain new Sunday service under our proposal. One of our themes is to just make more consistent service. And in many cases, we had routes that maybe didn't operate on Sundays or their service would dramatically drop off. So we're proposing to keep the 51 the same, but it would gain new Sunday service. Next slide, please.

56
00:22:33,254 --> 00:23:01,773
This shows South Brookline and then other parts of Boston that I won't be talking about here. But you can see the continuation of the 51 into South Brookline. And as I mentioned, that area will be getting a new Sunday service. But overall, Brookline is getting a substantial amount of new service. Overall, this plan represents a 25% increase in the amount of service that we're providing.

57
00:23:01,773 --> 00:23:24,757
And whether that is on evening service or weekend service or any of these kind of consolidations to create much more high frequency service. And that's something that we're really very excited about providing in Brooklyn. Next slide, please. Cambridge also has a lot of high frequency service. So it already today.

58
00:23:24,993 --> 00:23:46,087
had a lot of high frequency service. Today there's five key bus routes. Those would be the 1, the 71, the 73, the 66, and the 77. And aside from that 66 change over by the Longwood Medical Area, those routes are largely the same with the better off-peak frequency because we're promoting

59
00:23:46,087 --> 00:24:01,932
As a category, all of our key bus routes to be every 15 minutes or better, rather than every 20 so that means usually late at night, or maybe on weekends that we are proposing a frequency improvement for those routes, but we're actually proposing five.

60
00:24:01,932 --> 00:24:19,178
new routes that would come into Cambridge as well, and you can see there's quite a lot of those thick blue lines if you look at the map. So going through, we have one connection from the south that we're calling the T39,

61
00:24:19,718 --> 00:24:48,490
This would be an extension of today's 39 bus, which starts at Forest Hills and travels through Jamaica Plain. We're proposing that where the 39 gets to Brigham Circle, it would, instead of paralleling the E line all the way into Copley, we propose that it would diverge from the E line. It would offer a transfer to the E line at Brigham Circle for anyone wishing to continue to the Back Bay or Copley areas.

62
00:24:48,862 --> 00:25:13,178
But then it would travel through the Longwood Medical Area and sort of, I'll call it a mashup with today's 47 if you're familiar with that. So it would connect over by the BU Bridge, connect over by BU campus through Cambridge Port to Central Square. And then actually we would continue it beyond Central Square to Union Square in Somerville and up to Porter Square in Cambridge.

63
00:25:14,343 --> 00:25:42,608
So this is probably the biggest departure from an existing very heavily used route. But one of the things that we've been trying to do is figure out how to use our bus resources most effectively and to compliment our rapid transit networks rather than duplicate the rapid transit network. So what this does, although it does require a transfer for some folks who are traveling from JP and other areas into the Back Bay and Copley area,

64
00:25:42,608 --> 00:26:11,617
At the same time, it's making a far more direct trip for folks from JP who might be going to Cambridge or Somerville or to Red Line locations. And then similarly, it provides a direct connection for anyone coming from Somerville Ave, parts of Somerville and Cambridge, traveling into the Longwood Medical Area toward BU. So we see that there's a lot of value, and especially with the barrier posed by the Charles River,

65
00:26:11,617 --> 00:26:24,458
This provides another opportunity to add a sort of crosstown connection to our existing crosstown connections that we already have with the Mass Ave Bridge, the One Bus, and other Charles River crossings.

66
00:26:26,078 --> 00:26:42,835
Another group that we're proposing for that would be new for Cambridge would be the T70 bus. This is modeled off of today's route 70, though it simplifies it a bit so it would be

67
00:26:43,291 --> 00:27:08,165
all day high frequency with every 15 minutes or better from Waltham Centre into Kendal. So today, sometimes the bus goes to Cedarwood and sometimes the bus goes to Marketplace Drive in Waltham and sometimes it goes to Waltham Centre. So we're proposing that the high frequency portion of the route would be to Waltham Centre, which means that we would actually

68
00:27:08,165 --> 00:27:25,765
drop the portion of the route in Cedarwood and restructure the portion of the route out to the Market Basket in Waltham. At the same time, we're also proposing to extend the route to Kendall Square in Cambridge. One of the things that we've been trying to do

69
00:27:25,765 --> 00:27:49,171
kind of thematically with this plan is to connect some parts of our network that just for historic reasons hadn't been well connected. For example, the Longwood Medical Area, Kendall Square, and South Boston Waterfront. You know, some of these are very large regional employment destinations, and we see that there were, you know, lots of

70
00:27:49,340 --> 00:28:17,858
folks traveling to these areas from all over the network. So pulling in a connection to Kendall direct from the West. And while we recognize this does overlap with the red line, but we saw that there was a lot of trip making happening in that quarter. And also it happens to be the peak load point of the red line. So that seemed a reasonable place to have that overlap. Also, we have the T96 bus. This becomes,

71
00:28:18,972 --> 00:28:38,446
a very exciting crosstown connection linking together the red line, the green line, and the orange line to the north of Boston. We're proposing to restructure today's 96, make it a little bit more direct in the sort of area near Tufts so that it can pass

72
00:28:38,716 --> 00:29:04,670
by on College Avenue directly in front of the future Medford-Tufts station. So it would start at Porter Square, travel up to Davis, like where you can see the existing 96 operates today, and it would travel up through Ball Square, past Medford-Tufts, and actually, unlike today's 96, which starts and ends in Medford Square, it would continue all the way up to Malden Center, sort of a mashup

73
00:29:04,872 --> 00:29:28,970
with the top half of the number 101 bus. And the entirety of that quarter would be promoted to all day high frequency to create a really strong sort of northern crosstown connection. If you recall seeing the map earlier of the existing key bus routes, you can see that it was very slim in terms of the numbers of routes

74
00:29:28,970 --> 00:29:55,582
kind of to the north of Boston. So this is an exciting addition to the number of high-frequency services that we have. We also have the T101 bus. On this map you can see it starts at Kendall MIT and this would connect over to Lechmere, which would be the first time that we have a sort of direct MBTA operated connection. I know the

75
00:29:55,582 --> 00:30:20,135
the EZ-Ride operates there and then this would continue past New Lechmere Station over the Gilmore Bridge into Charlestown and actually would serve area along Main Street in Charlestown and then continue past Sullivan up into Somerville and Medford and we have some maps that show that so I'll speak about that a little

76
00:30:20,135 --> 00:30:48,957
bit more on the next slide. And then lastly, the T109 bus is the fifth high frequency connection. So this is the route that you get when you combine the top half of the number 86 bus that I mentioned was sort of separated from the rest of the 86 with the 109 bus. The 109 is slated to be, that's coming from Everett, that's up and down Broadway from Linden Square.

77
00:30:48,957 --> 00:31:05,545
down through Everett and currently terminates at Sullivan. We proposed that that would get mashed up with the top half of the 86 to create an all day high frequency connection from Everett through Sullivan and continuing on to Union Square

78
00:31:05,545 --> 00:31:23,348
in Somerville and on to Harvard in Cambridge. So this is exciting because today, you know, everyone coming from Everett is sort of forced to transfer to the Orange Line, whereas this is trying to knit together suburbs that are very close to each other.

79
00:31:23,348 --> 00:31:45,978
and allow for more opportunities for direct connections and through trips. And also just opportunities for better frequency because today the 86, you know, sometimes on Sundays it can be every 30 or 40 minutes, but there's really quite a bit of trip-making happening in that sort of Harvard and Union Square to Sullivan Square corridor.

80
00:31:47,800 --> 00:32:14,564
So overall, you can see there's a lot more high frequency service coming to Cambridge. There's also some changes to the moderate frequency routes as well. There are a few changes that I'll point out. So at Kendall, you can see that we have a proposed extension of the Route 55. This is sort of a combination of two routes. It's taking today's 55, which is

81
00:32:14,564 --> 00:32:25,347
route that connects the West Fenway neighborhood in Boston to the Green Line. We're proposing that would continue to do so at a future accessible Heinz

82
00:32:25,769 --> 00:32:55,368
station, but we're proposing that this would then cross over the Mass Ave Bridge and continue to Kendall-MIT. This is sort of kind of filling in and taking the part of the role of the CT2 bus as a way to connect Red Line riders to the Longwood Medical Area, or at least some Red Line riders who might not be within walking distance of, say, the new T39 bus that's also proposed to connect other parts over to the Longwood Medical Area.

83
00:32:56,532 --> 00:33:15,500
There's also a change to the 64 bus, it would be made more consistent. Today, sometimes it goes to central University Park area and sometimes it goes to Kendall, we would make that change consistent seven days a week.

84
00:33:15,500 --> 00:33:38,213
and would always go to Kendall. There's also a proposed change that's contingent on once the future west station is completed in Austin, the 64 would be slated to use that new station, but we have some alternate routes that would be in place until that future station is completed. There's also a proposed route change to the 83

85
00:33:38,905 --> 00:34:03,408
Today, that is connecting from Russell Field down Rindge Avenue and connects over eventually to Central Square. That is proposed to instead be extended to Kendall-MIT to provide more opportunities to get from sort of the Port neighborhood and Inman Square over to Kendall because

86
00:34:03,408 --> 00:34:25,447
Again, for historic reasons, many, many routes have long terminated Central Square, but historically there hadn't been as much development as there is today in Kendall, so we're trying to reorient some of those services over to the Kendall area. And I'll flag that the 68 route is proposed to become a peak only service.

